ting multiple forums for  discussion discussing debating questions  of fundamental rights you don't need you  don't know anymore whether you need to  go to your local court or whether you  really have to go to the Constitutional  Court and what exactly is going to be  the dividing line that is one of the  criticisms that one can hear indeed okay  so do you think all of this is will will  lead to a power grab by the part of the  Union  I wouldn't necessarily think so first of  all and maybe I should go a little bit  into the cases that the European Court  of Justice has decided during this first  time there has been a first series of  cases in which the court has been very  cautious when it came precisely to the  rights of Nationals of the European  Union European Union citizens who were  married or had some family members who  were third country nationals so a little  bit the situation like the lady from the  Philippines that we're talking about in  the beginning and they're curiously the  court of justice has come to say that  those third country nationals who want  to remain with the Union citizen in the  state of origin of nationality of that  Union citizen are not necessarily within  the scope of European Union law so under  the Lisbon Treaty they can not invoke  the right to family life in order to  obtain the right to live and work in the  member state of origin of their Union  family member  which maybe a bit of reversal of the  earlier case law that I've described so  that was pretty cautious I would say on  the other hand there is one case in  which the court of justice has been  quite expansive and that case is I think  worth going into for a second it is the  case of a Swedish fisherman who  apparently cheated on his tax return  income tax on the one hand and  value-added tax which is some kind of a  sales tax on the other so of course the  Swedish authorities went after him and  inflicted first an administrative  sanction on him and then started also  criminal proceedings and during those  criminal proceedings the national court  in charge of that in Sweden was  wondering whether as a matter of  European Union law the person in  question didn't enjoy the right not to  be tried or prosecuted and sentenced  twice a right which is known in the US  as the prohibition of double jeopardy  right so in order for the court to  address that it first had to say that  that situation was actually within the  scope of European Union law and now one  must know that income tax legislation is  still completely a matter of national  law so that for that part he was  certainly not within the scope of union  law by contrast that you added tax this  sales tax is actually in substance  harmonized at European Union level but  when it comes to sanctions all that the  rule says that the member states have to  ensure that the tax is effectively  collected and they have to prevent  evasion so the question was is that  enough to say that a system of sanctions  administrative and criminal sanctions  for avoidance evasion of value-added tax  came within the scope of European Union  law and the court thought it did  I don't want to go into the details of  the reasoning which some commentators  have criticized as actually not being  entirely convincing but they would like  to underline the importance of the  consequences what this means is that  whenever nowadays a claim and the  European Union law is enforced with the  system of procedural law and sometimes  criminal law in a Member State those  procedural and criminal rules might come  within the scope of European Union law  and might therefore be checked against  the Union fundamental rights and  something that that actually goes very  far in particular the Federal  Constitutional Court in Germany has  actually sent a very clear signal to the  Court of Justice in a ruling in which it  said that it thought that what the court  had done there needed to be understood  very narrowly and limited to the  circumstances of the specific case it  seems very complicated to me that the  way that things are set up where you  have basically the different member  states have their own legal system which  then also plays into the European Union  legal system am i understanding that  correctly absolutely yes and that I mean  that does create a lot of questions I  mean let me let me just look at some of  them one question is of course the  question of democracy right normally you  would expect that you should apply the  set of fundamental rights basic rights  the right not to be tried twice etc  which comes from the polity that has  actually adopted the bulk of the rules  that you're going to apply right it  should be the same that that would be as  a tendency more democratic now the court  has tried to get around that by saying  you can actually apply both yeah as long  as you don't impair the effectiveness of  European Union law you can apply both a  national fundamental right and a union  fundamental right complicated as you  suggest  but one can maybe envisage that in  situations in which we're only talking  about a fundamental right coming from  national law and a similar right which  comes from union law because what you  could do then would be to say well let  the person benefit from the better  protection yeah but as soon as you've  got a situation in which several  fundamental rights of various parties in  the dispute compete it becomes extremely  difficult to see what better protection  is going to mean let's imagine a  situation in which one person wants to  publish something and relies on his or  her freedom of speech and the other  person thinks that this is actually  something very private which should be  protected and relies on the right to  privacy right so then in that case what  is better protection going to be the  better protection of the freedom of  speech is going to be the worst  protection of the right to privacy of  course and in that situation  simply giving preference to European  Union law in a situation which is  otherwise not that closely connected to  European Union law may not be the ideal  solution solution so the question really  is what kind of federal system of  protection of fundamental rights do we  want to create in particular one might  think that there is some value in having  diversity at the beginning when you've  got a certain approach towards let's say  gay marriage in one state and another  approach in another state they can sort  of experiment and the debate that is  being held in the various Member States  may then inform and sharpen the debate  at the central level which is something  that we have seen in the US for instance  well so the question is whether in a  situation like that in which society is  in the process of evolution it is really  a good idea that the court of justice  attracts all that towards it and rules  on it immediately before giving a chance  to these individual different attitudes  and approaches to develop bearing in  mind also that the European Court of  Justice  is not a specialized human-rights cord  it also does many other things okay  so let's take a look at the issues that  you think are rise from the courts  approach that you've just described yes  well there are there are the ones that  I've just mentioned here and I would  maybe like to add also that sort of a  different approach which would speak in  favor of protecting fundamental union  fundamental rights in the Member States  also more broadly might be the situation  in which for one reason or another  fundamental rights protection in the  member a member state fails it just  stops protecting certain fundamental  rights properly in a situation like that  which one may hope will remain entirely  theoretical of course in the European  Union you might want to use Union  fundamental rights at least as a  fallback and that is something which may  have been on the courts mind also when  it decided to draw the scope of Union  fundamental rights more broadly but in  the immediate future what I think the  court will need to do is to clarify its  case law more the tendency is the one  concerning Union citizens on the one  hand and the one concerning this Swedish  taxpayer on the other seem difficult to  reconcile so the court is probably going  to get more questions from national  court national courts and it will have  to refine its case law and to engage in  this judicial and maybe sometimes also  extra judicial dialogue with national  courts in order to convince them so I  think this is certainly going to be a  space to be watched in the near future  right it sounds like it will be a  process that evolves over time  absolutely  well thank you very much for being here  with us today and sharing some of your  work thank you subpoena pick up for more  information about mr. Shima and his  research please visit our website at  yale.edu slash McMillan report be sure  to join us again for another episode of  the McMillan  port made possible through funding from  the Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center  for International and area studies at  Yale
 

